course: dictionary
lesson: 9158
title: Snot and its polite word
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: «Snot» is an informal word. People use it in chatty talk, but it is a little rude, so in school writing you will often see the polite word «mucus». Context clues tell you what it means: «Green or yellow snot is coloured by white blood cells fighting the infection» shows that snot comes from your body and can change colour.
explain: The word has a second meaning. A snot is a rude, proud, cheeky person who thinks they are better than others, and the adjective is «snotty». Read the clues around it: if a story says someone is boasting and looking down on others, then calling them a snot is about their behaviour, not their nose.
explain: Check which meaning fits by swapping in your idea. «Antibiotics kill bacteria, not viruses, so they do nothing for a cold, even when the snot turns green.» Here it must mean the stuff from the nose, because it is linked to a cold. Try it: "even when the nose stuff turns green" still makes sense.
fact: Snot is the slimy stuff, called mucus in science, that comes out of your nose.
fact: «Snot» is informal, so use the polite word «mucus» in school writing.
fact: Mucus traps dust and germs, and helps protect your body.
fact: «Snotty» can describe a nose full of snot, or a person who is rude and proud.
fact: «Catch it, bin it, kill it» means sneeze into a tissue, throw it away and wash your hands.
exercise: «Green or yellow snot is coloured by white blood cells fighting the infection.» What is snot in this sentence? | A type of tissue | A kind of green paint | *The slimy stuff that comes out of your nose | A rude, proud person
exercise: Ella says, «Ben is such a snot, he thinks he is better than everyone.» What does snot mean here? | *A rude, proud, cheeky person | A small insect | A kind of soup | The slimy stuff from your nose
exercise: What is the polite science word for snot? | A sort of tissue paper | A cold drink | *Mucus | A type of bacteria
test: In hygiene lessons, what does «Catch it, bin it, kill it» tell you to do? | Keep the used tissue in your pocket | Hold your nose and run outside | *Sneeze into a tissue, throw it away and wash your hands | Blow your nose on your sleeve
test: Why should an ill person see a doctor if they stay ill a long time? | Because antibiotics cure every cold | Because green snot always means a cold | *Because they might need help, whatever colour their snot is | Because snot is always a sign of a serious illness
exam: Which best describes the healthy job of mucus? | *It traps dust and germs and helps protect your body | It makes you sneeze all day for no reason | It is only there for jokes | It turns your nose green on purpose
